---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About — Starisk"
  description="Why Starisk exists, what the Coursera headline misses, and what the 29 calibration courses mean."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <header>
      <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">About</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-base-content/75">
        Starisk is a small research project about the part of a Coursera course that the
        headline rating does not capture.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-base-content/85">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Why I built it</h2>
      <p>
        I started this while choosing my next course. I had taken a few <strong>4.9</strong>{' '}
        courses that were bad, some <strong>4.7</strong> courses that were good, and{' '}
        <strong>4.8</strong> courses that were excellent. The headline was always above 4.5,
        so it was not separating them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reviews were what separated them. Specifically, the ones other learners had upvoted
        carried more signal about whether a course was worth the time than the average did.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tested the idea on a deliberate streak of good courses and bad ones. It kept holding:
        the upvoted reviews were pointing at a nearly independent dimension that the headline
        was not measuring. I suspected the headline was doing commercial work as much as
        measurement. Starisk does not claim to know intent — it just measures the gap.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-base-content/85">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Why publish it</h2>
      <p>
        When I was choosing, it was hard to find useful information about individual courses.
        The popular ones had Reddit threads. The niche, specialized, or newly published ones
        often had almost nothing. I wanted to make the signal I was already using easier to
        find.
      </p>
      <p>
        I took many of my courses through Coursera's financial aid, so choosing carefully
        mattered before I applied. I wanted to know I would finish the course rather than
        merely tolerate it. Some of the best ones were as enjoyable as they were challenging.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is also a research and portfolio piece. The crawler, the scoring model, the
        calibration test, and the site are all part of the work — not just a chart with a
        logo on top.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-base-content/85">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">What the 29 means</h2>
      <p>
        I have completed 72 Coursera credentials (61 courses and 11 programs, plus 9
        from other serious platforms). The{" "}<strong>29</strong>{" "}
        on the <a class="link link-hover" href="/method">Method</a> page
        are the subset I re-rated blind from memory to test the model. They are a calibration
        sample, not my transcript. The full list is on
        <a class="link link-hover" href="/background">Background</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="rounded-box bg-base-200 p-4 text-sm">
        That sample is small, and the Method page says so: n = 29, with roughly ±0.15 of
        uncertainty. It is directional evidence for a principled fix, not a proof.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-base-content/85">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">What Starisk is, and isn't</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
          <h3 class="font-display font-bold text-success">It is</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 ml-4 list-disc space-y-1 text-sm text-base-content/80">
            <li>What the most-upvoted written reviews say.</li>
            <li>A second number to read next to Coursera's.</li>
            <li>An honest record of where the two disagree.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
          <h3 class="font-display font-bold text-error">It is not</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 ml-4 list-disc space-y-1 text-sm text-base-content/80">
            <li>Objective quality, or a verdict on the teaching.</li>
            <li>An instructor rating.</li>
            <li>A product with accounts, or an affiliation with Coursera.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-base-content/85">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Who</h2>
      <p>
        I'm Matías Agelvis. You can find me at{' '}
        <a
          class="link link-hover"
          href="https://matiasagelvis.com"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">matiasagelvis.com</a
        >, or write to{' '}
        <a class="link link-hover" href="mailto:hello@matiasagelvis.com"
          >hello@matiasagelvis.com</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        The code, the data pipeline, and the calibration notes are in the{' '}
        <a
          class="link link-hover"
          href="https://github.com/MatiasAgelvis/starisk"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">repository</a
        >. If you want the technical version of this page, read the{' '}
        <a class="link link-hover" href="/method">method</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
